{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011ChHDooSojAMeSSkD48FhN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This isn't a bug, so I didn't write a repro script.\n\nIn 2026, Toronto's clocks went back an hour at 02:00 on Sunday 1 November: daylight time (EDT, UTC−4) ended and standard time (EST, UTC−5) began. That falls inside your night, so 02:00 to 03:00 happened twice:\n\n- Bedtime 23:00 EDT on Sat 31 Oct = 03:00 UTC\n- Wake 07:00 EST on Sun 1 Nov = 12:00 UTC\n- Real time between them: **9 hours**\n\nThe clock only moved forward 8 hours, but 9 hours actually passed. NightJar works it out correctly: `Night.duration` (`src/nightjar/models.py:52`) converts both times to UTC before subtracting. I ran that night through the real code and got offsets of −4h at bedtime, −5h at wake, and a duration of `9:00:00`.\n\nA naive wall-clock calculation would show 8h. That would be the bug, and the project notes warn against it.\n\nThe opposite happens on the spring-forward night (8 March 2027): 23:00 to 07:00 will show 7h, and that will be correct too."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":289,"cache_read_input_tokens":23915,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":289},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"586b6e33-c21a-4dc0-9103-31b527ca2c71","uuid":"585e6bfe-394d-4a9a-9828-b92326b81299","timestamp":"2026-11-22T03:01:52.970Z","request_id":"req_011ChHDooJck2nnmVJYU8n6v"}
